 			<p>As she departed, <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mr.</abbr> Dashwood put up his feet, with the graceful remark, “Poor and proud, as usual, but she’ll do.”</p>
 			<p>Following <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mr.</abbr> Dashwood’s directions, and making <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mrs.</abbr> Northbury her model, Jo rashly took a plunge into the frothy sea of sensational literature; but, thanks to the life-preserver thrown her by a friend, she came up again, not much the worse for her ducking.</p>
 			<p>Like most young scribblers, she went abroad for her characters and scenery; and banditti, counts, gypsies, nuns, and duchesses appeared upon her stage, and played their parts with as much accuracy and spirit as could be expected. Her readers were not particular about such trifles as grammar, punctuation, and probability, and <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mr.</abbr> Dashwood graciously permitted her to fill his columns at the lowest prices, not thinking it necessary to tell her that the real cause of his hospitality was the fact that one of his hacks, on being offered higher wages, had basely left him in the lurch.</p>
